 by heavy deformation in that region, but they are also characterized as defects associated with rolling in the same region; when two-phase-region rolling is not carried out, separations are few. In fractured Charpy specimens, separations appear in both longitudinal and transverse directions, and through-thickness embrittlement is considered their main cause. They can occur with banded structure, and chromium suppresses banded structure to prevent their occurrence. When separations are few, they have little effect on transition temperature [Data: Entities (994, 1183, 689); Relationships (2546, 2619)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"TRANSITION TEMPERATURE is the central toughness property and is multiply controlled\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The TRANSITION TEMPERATURE (T_TS) is a toughness-related property at which a transition occurs. It is affected by alloying elements, precipitates, chemical composition, grain size and refinement, strengthening factors, subgrains, dislocation hardening, precipitation hardening, deformation, and separations. It decreases with an increasing number of separations and is determined mainly by precipitation and dislocation hardening, grain refinement, and separations. Controlled-rolled steel exhibits a lower transition temperature than conventionally hot-rolled, normalized, or quenched and tempered steel. Hot-rolled strip usually has a much higher transition temperature than plate because of precipitation hardening and few separations [Data: Entities (66, 65, 652, 653, 1035); Relationships (1335, 1336, 2277)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The number of separations quantitatively lowers absorbed energy and transition temperature\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NUMBER OF SEPARATIONS, denoted n_S, is the count of separations in a Charpy specimen. The number of separations decreases with increasing temperature. Increasing n_S decreases both absorbed energy and transition temperature, and the transition temperature decreases