 temperature compared with hot-rolled strip. The transition temperature is usually much higher for hot-rolled strip than for plate. Separations appear in fractured Charpy specimens in the longitudinal direction and in the transverse direction, where very low transition temperatures are achieved. The transition temperature differs between the L and Z directions by more than 100 K [Data: Entities (3, 1035, 1183, 689); Relationships (750, 2277, 2546, 1347)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Strengthening factors and hardening mechanisms raise transition temperature except grain refinement\",\n      \"explanation\": \"STRENGTHENING FACTORS raise the transition temperature, with grain refinement as the only exception. Hardening due to precipitation, dislocations, and similar mechanisms is one of the contradictory factors determining transition temperature. The transition temperature is determined by hardening due to precipitation, dislocations, etc., and this relationship is explicitly represented in the data [Data: Entities (871, 1189); Relationships (1740, 2569)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedures exist to eliminate separations and manage banded structure\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Procedures have been proposed to eliminate separations. SEPARATIONS can occur with banded structure, and chromium suppresses banded structure to prevent their occurrence. When two-phase-region rolling is not carried out, separations are few, and when separations are few they have little effect on transition temperature. These points indicate mitigation routes focused on processing and composition [Data: Entities (994, 1206, 66); Relationships (2619)].\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is moderate-high because separations and transition temperature directly affect steel toughness, product qualification, and industrial rolling practice, although the community is technical rather than threat-oriented.\"\n}", "refusal": null